    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear directive: maximize stolen base upside against exploitable pitching. **Corbin Carroll** (78.77) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) headline our attack, as both benefit immensely from high-efficiency matchups against lefties with inflated xERA marks. **Brice Turang** (71.96) is our engine at second base; his projected lead-off spot against a high-delivery-time starter like Quantrill creates a prime environment for base-thievery. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** (70.90) serves as our high-leverage utility play, as the matchup against Bennett allows his elite speed to negate the disadvantage of batting seventh in the order. \n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Mickey Moniak** (55.11) and **Austin Riley** (59.88) on the fringe of the starting rotation, both just narrowly edging out potential alternatives. **Mickey Moniak** faced a significant platoon penalty today, but his scoring floor was bolstered by the weakness of P\u00e9rez, keeping him in the lineup despite the downgrade. Meanwhile, **Max Muncy** (59.99) remains a high-variance asset whose favorable park factors pushed him past the 50-point threshold, though we are monitoring his lineup confirmation closely. We are currently navigating a depleted bench; with stalwarts like **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** on the IL, and **Vinnie Pasquantino** out of today's lineup, we are relying on internal depth like **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte** to keep our daily efficiency ceiling intact. \n\nRisk management for today centers on our reliance on several \"Lineup Pending\" assignments. If **Brice Turang** or **Max Muncy** move down in the order, their efficiency scores\u2014already sensitive to lineup placement\u2014will crater. Furthermore, while **Gabriel Moreno** benefits from a massive SP skill boost against Wilkinson, his performance is highly susceptible to the 3% park drag, making his 66.09 score a \"Strong\" play we must watch closely. We remain confident that our emphasis on speed-focused matchups will help us gain ground in the 5x5 Roto standings, provided **Isaac Paredes** (51.13) can navigate his own challenging SP matchup without further efficiency decay.",
